<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>netstandard2.0</TargetFramework>
    <IncludeBuildOutput>false</IncludeBuildOutput>
    <GenerateDependencyFile>false</GenerateDependencyFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU5128;NU5100</NoWarn>
    <PackageId>TINS.Native.Desktop</PackageId>
    <Description>Meta-package bundling TINS.Native.win-x64, TINS.Native.linux-x64, TINS.Native.osx-x64, and TINS.Native.osx-arm64, so a multi-platform consumer (e.g. a cross-targeted test project) can pull in native FFTW/OpenBLAS assets for every desktop RID with a single reference instead of adding each RID package individually. A single-RID deployment should still reference just its own TINS.Native.&lt;rid&gt; package directly -- this carries no native content of its own.</Description>
    <PackageTags>Neuroscience, Native, FFTW, OpenBLAS, Desktop</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- $(Version) isn't usable here: MinVer computes it via a build target that runs
         *after* restore, but restore needs a concrete version right now to resolve these
         PackageReferences (chicken and egg).

         This used to be a floating version ("0.0.0-alpha.1.*"), which resolves at RESTORE
         time to the highest matching version in the feed, but a floating spec has no
         publishable meaning in a nuspec, so `dotnet pack` is forced to collapse it down to
         whatever concrete version happened to resolve *at pack time* and bakes that in as
         the minimum. Every time Desktop got repacked, that minimum silently ratcheted up to
         the latest sibling version, so as soon as an older intermediate version of a
         sibling package (e.g. 1.12) got pruned from a downstream feed that only mirrors the
         latest, consumers hit NU1603 ("dependency version not found, nearest match used":
         benign, but noisy, and a symptom of a floor that shouldn't have moved).

         A plain (non-floating) version string is valid nuspec syntax as-is and NuGet
         already treats it as a minimum-inclusive bound ">= X", so pinning to the fixed,
         low "0.0.0-alpha.1" floor here means Desktop is satisfied by ANY sibling package
         ever built in this alpha.1 family, regardless of its specific height, without the
         floor moving on every repack. Restore still picks up whatever's actually present in
         the configured feed (currently the latest of each), so local dev/testing behaves
         the same as before; only the *baked minimum* changes. Update this (and
         MinVerDefaultPreReleaseIdentifiers in Directory.Build.props) together if that
         identifier ever changes (e.g. once real release tags start). -->
    <PackageReference Include="TINS.Native.win-x64" Version="0.0.0-alpha.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="TINS.Native.linux-x64" Version="0.0.0-alpha.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="TINS.Native.osx-x64" Version="0.0.0-alpha.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="TINS.Native.osx-arm64" Version="0.0.0-alpha.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
